## What Was Demonstrated

At this point the following has been established:

| Test | Result |
|---|---|
| Service account exists | ✅ |
| Account is enabled | ✅ |
| Account has registered SPN(s) | ✅ |
| Standard domain user can request TGS | ✅ |
| KDC issues service ticket | ✅ |
| Ticket appears in Kerberos cache | ✅ |
| Ticket encryption type can be identified | ✅ |
| Crackable `$krb5tgs$` material extracted | ❌ Not performed |
| Offline password cracking performed | ❌ Not performed |
| Service-account password recovered | ❌ Not demonstrated |

---

## Attack Path

Conceptually, Kerberoasting follows this process:

```text
Standard Domain User
        │
        ▼
Enumerate SPN-Bearing User Accounts
        │
        ▼
Request TGS for Registered SPN
        │
        ▼
Domain Controller / KDC Issues TGS
        │
        ▼
Service Ticket Cached Locally
        │
        ▼
Extract Encrypted Ticket Material
        │
        ▼
Convert to Offline Password-Audit Format
        │
        ▼
Offline Password Guessing
        │
        ▼
Password Recovered?
```

This exercise validated the workflow through **successful TGS issuance and local ticket caching**.

---

## Security Impact

The existence of an SPN is not itself a vulnerability.

The concern is that user-based service accounts with SPNs can expose Kerberos service-ticket material to authenticated domain users. If the underlying service-account password is weak enough to be recovered through an offline password attack, the attacker may obtain the service account's credentials.

The resulting impact depends on the privileges assigned to that identity.

Examples include:

```text
Recovered Service Account
        │
        ├── Application access
        │
        ├── Database access
        │
        ├── Administrative access to servers
        │
        ├── AD permissions
        │
        └── Kerberos delegation rights
```

Service accounts configured with delegation or elevated permissions therefore warrant additional scrutiny.

---

## Key Takeaway

Finding an SPN does **not** mean the service-account password has been compromised.

The important distinction is:

```text
SPN discovered
      ↓
TGS successfully requested
      ↓
Kerberoast prerequisite confirmed
      ↓
Password strength still unknown
```

A successful TGS request demonstrates that the account can participate in the Kerberoasting attack path, while actual password recovery depends on the account's password strength and requires a separate offline password audit.

